{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>But among all the children of Israel, etc.</b> One needs to know the intention here, for if it were to say that not a person among them would die, it should have stated explicitly: ״וְלֹא יָמוּת מִבְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, etc." "Or perhaps the intention in the arrangement of the statement follows the saying of Amram, of blessed memory (Bava Kamma 60a): dogs howl — the angel of death is in the city. Therefore He said <b>but among all the children of Israel</b>, etc., so that you should know that not a person among them would die — that no dog would sharpen its tongue there. From this you learn that no destroyer appeared among them, and this is His saying <b>that you may know that YHWH makes a distinction</b>." "<b>Further,</b> the intention was to hint to him that specifically in a place where all the children of Israel would be — meaning, all of them together would be Israel — ״לֹא יֶחֱרַץ כֶּלֶב״, etc.; this is a sign for you that no stranger is among them. But if they would not all be children of Israel and there would be an Egyptian among them, a dog would sharpen its tongue. From this you learn that a destroyer would be seen there. And the intention in this is to tell him that cunning would not avail the firstborn to escape, for even if the Egyptian firstborn hide among Israel, they would not escape by this means — and so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Exodus Rabbah 18:2)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:26:39.783949574Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}